---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { data, AXIS_ORDER, axisMeta, suitesById } from '../lib/data.js';

const scoringLabel = (s) =>
  s === 'claude-judge-0-20' ? 'LLM-judged 0–20 per test against a fixed rubric' : s;

// axis blurbs — what each axis measures, in the proving-ground voice
const blurbs = {
  g: 'The baseline gauntlet. A cross-section of the workloads a local daily driver actually gets handed: dev/ops scripting, document processing, professional content production. If a model can’t hold up here, the rest is academic.',
  a: 'Can the model behave inside an agent harness? Tool-call formatting, protocol adherence, and robustness when state gets messy. A model that free-styles its tool calls fails in production no matter how smart the prose is.',
  u: 'Vision-grounded document understanding: transcription accuracy, field and checkbox extraction, table reasoning — including a deliberately nasty real-degraded tier of scans and handwriting that separates demo-grade OCR from usable OCR.',
  n: 'Long-context retrieval under adversarial conditions: needles, distractors, ops-log reasoning, faithful summarization, and multi-needle MRCR. Scores here reflect what the model does with a genuinely full context window, not a marketing number.',
  t: null, // pending — custom section below
  l: 'One-shot builds that must actually run. Output is executed in a real browser and verified at runtime — not just read by a judge.',
  e: 'Coding depth beyond scripting: concurrency, algorithms, security review, performance work, refactoring under constraint, and test writing. The suite probes whether a model can engineer, not just autocomplete.',
  tp: null, // custom section below
};

const anchorFor = (key) => `axis-${key}`;
const unmappedSuites = data.suites.filter(
  (s) => !data.axes.some((a) => a.suites.includes(s.id))
);
---
<Base
  title="Methodology"
  description="How GAUNTLET Bench scores local models: eight axes, fixed rubrics, runtime verification, and a contamination policy we're proud of."
>
  <div class="page-head">
    <p class="kicker">Methodology</p>
    <h1>The GAUNTLET</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Eight axes, one per letter. Every score on this site is produced by the same pipeline,
      on the same host ({data.host.hw}, {data.host.runner}), from versioned test suites with
      fixed judging rubrics. An axis score is the points a model earned across that axis&rsquo;s
      suites divided by the maximum possible, on a 0&ndash;100 scale &mdash; unrun tests count
      as zero, so partial runs can&rsquo;t inflate a score.
    </p>
  </div>

  {AXIS_ORDER.map((key) => {
    const axis = axisMeta[key];
    const suites = axis.suites.map((id) => suitesById[id]);
    return (
      <section class="axis-section" id={anchorFor(key)}>
        <div class="axis-head">
          <span class={key === 't' ? 'letter-badge pending' : 'letter-badge'}>{axis.letter}</span>
          <div>
            <h2>{axis.name}</h2>
            <span class="axis-key">axis: {axis.letter} · {key === 't' ? 'stall census v1' : key === 'tp' ? 'measured, not judged' : `${suites.length} suite${suites.length === 1 ? '' : 's'}`}</span>
          </div>
        </div>

        {key === 't' ? (
          <>
            <p>
              The Thinking axis measures reasoning-budget behavior: how many reasoning tokens a
              model burns to get to an answer, and whether it stalls in its own chain of thought
              and never emits one. This matters enormously for agentic use &mdash; a model that
              thinks for sixteen thousand tokens and returns an empty answer is a dead turn to
              any harness, whatever its accuracy elsewhere.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Scoring (v1, stall census):</strong> we scan every archived run &mdash;
              1,000+ per-test results across all suites &mdash; and count <em>stalls</em>
              (reasoning emitted, final answer empty) and <em>runaways</em> (token cap hit with
              no stop). The score is 100&nbsp;&times;&nbsp;(1&nbsp;&minus;&nbsp;(stalls&nbsp;+&nbsp;runaways)&nbsp;/&nbsp;tests
              observed). A model needs at least 15 observed tests to be scored; below that the
              axis shows <em>n/d</em> rather than a number built on thin data. Repeat runs count
              as repeat observations &mdash; this is a behavior census, not a leaderboard average.
              Each model&rsquo;s page shows its raw census: stalls, observed tests, and reasoning-token
              appetite (mean and max). Non-reasoning models can score here too: a stall-free record
              is a stall-free record. Effort-setting sensitivity is collected and will fold into a
              v2 of this metric.
            </p>
          </>
        ) : key === 'tp' ? (
          <>
            <p>
              Throughput is the one axis that isn&rsquo;t judged &mdash; it&rsquo;s measured.
              We record mean generation speed (tokens/second) across every suite a model runs,
              on this host, and normalize against the fastest model tested so the axis lands on
              the same 0&ndash;100 scale as the rest of the radar.
            </p>
            <p>
              Speed is reported per suite on each model page too, because it varies: long-context
              work is dramatically slower than short-form generation, and that difference is part
              of the evidence.
            </p>
          </>
        ) : (
          <>
            <p>{blurbs[key]}</p>
            {key === 'l' && (
              <p>
                <strong>Why runtime verification exists:</strong> static judge scores overstate
                reality. A judge reading source code will happily award points to a build that
                throws on load. The Live axis closes that gap &mdash; each artifact is opened in
                a browser and verified to actually run before it scores. What survives contact
                with a runtime is the score.
              </p>
            )}
            {suites.map((s) => (
              <div>
                <p class="suite-line">
                  <strong>{s.name}</strong> <span class="chip">{s.id}</span> &mdash; {s.test_count} tests,
                  {' '}{scoringLabel(s.scoring)} (max {s.test_count * s.max_score_per_test} points).
                </p>
                <div class="cat-chips">
                  {s.categories.map((c) => <span class="chip">{c}</span>)}
                </div>
              </div>
            ))}
          </>
        )}
      </section>
    );
  })}

  <section class="callout" id="contamination">
    <h2>The contamination policy</h2>
    <p>
      Live test prompts, gold answers, and judge rubrics stay private. Not because the method is
      secret &mdash; this page describes it in full &mdash; but because the moment a benchmark&rsquo;s
      contents are public, models get trained on them and the benchmark stops measuring anything.
      Benchmark contamination is the defining failure mode of public leaderboards, and we opted out
      at inception.
    </p>
    <p>
      The trade is explicit and we think it&rsquo;s a good one:
    </p>
    <ul style="color:var(--text-dim);">
      <li>Suites are versioned. When a suite version is retired and superseded, it becomes a public worked example &mdash; prompts, rubric, and all.</li>
      <li>Any sample prompts shown on this site are already-public community prompts, never live suite content.</li>
      <li>Nothing from the live suites &mdash; prompts, gold answers, rubrics &mdash; goes into any training or fine-tuning dataset for any model this project will ever score.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This is a feature, not secrecy. A benchmark you can&rsquo;t train on is a benchmark you can trust.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Notes</h2>
    <ul>
      {unmappedSuites.length > 0 && (
        <li>
          {unmappedSuites.map((s) => `${s.name} (${s.id})`).join(', ')} {unmappedSuites.length === 1 ? 'is' : 'are'} run
          and reported on model pages but not yet mapped to an axis.
        </li>
      )}
      <li>All runs execute on a single controlled host: {data.host.name}, {data.host.hw}, {data.host.os}, served by {data.host.runner}. One standard, one machine &mdash; scores are comparable because nothing else varies.</li>
      <li>&ldquo;&mdash;&rdquo; on the leaderboard means a suite hasn&rsquo;t been run for that model yet, not that it scored zero.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Base>
